--TEST--
`"lit" as Core\Html\Markup` stays the lift `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape` owns, and is not the checked downcast every other `Core` class target now takes
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The lift builds a `Markup` out of bytes the developer wrote. Nothing is
// tested, because a source-literal `string` is trusted by the rule rather than
// by a descriptor comparison — which is what makes this the one `Core` target
// decided by a rule rather than by a test. A computed operand is refused where
// it is written, and `markup-refuses-a-computed-operand-and-a-half-markup-sum`
// is where that refusal is pinned.
Core\Html\Markup $m = "<b>bold</b>" as Core\Html\Markup;
echo Core\Html::toSource($m, "the case reads the bytes back"), "|\n";

// The downcast is the other shape, and it tests: the same operator over a
// `mixed` and a `Core` class with instances compares the value's runtime class
// against the descriptor the process publishes, then throws or hands the
// instance back.
mixed $date = Core\Time\Date::at(2026, 9, 17);
echo ($date as Core\Time\Date)->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "|\n";

// A lifted `Markup` reaches the test the same way any other instance does, so
// the two shapes agree about what the value is once it exists.
mixed $carrier = $m;
if ($carrier is Core\Html\Markup) {
    echo "a markup|\n";
}
--EXPECT--
<b>bold</b>|
2026-09-17|
a markup|
